    "design": "Randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial at 99 centers in North America (enrolled June 1999 through April 2001). It tested two questions in addition to aspirin: a clopidogrel loading dose before PCI, and 12 months of clopidogrel afterward. Main outcomes were the 1-year incidence of death, myocardial infarction (MI), or stroke in the intent-to-treat population, and the 28-day incidence of death, MI, or urgent target vessel revascularization in the per-protocol population.",
    "population": "2116 patients who were to undergo elective PCI or were deemed at high likelihood of undergoing PCI.",
    "intervention": "Clopidogrel 300-mg loading dose (n=1053) 3 to 24 hours before PCI, then clopidogrel 75 mg/d through day 28 and from day 29 through 12 months, plus aspirin.",
    "comparator": "Placebo (n=1063) 3 to 24 hours before PCI, then clopidogrel 75 mg/d through day 28 and placebo from day 29 through 12 months, plus aspirin.",
    "primaryResult": "At 1 year, long-term clopidogrel therapy was associated with a 26.9% relative reduction in death, MI, or stroke (95% CI 3.9% to 44.4%; P=.02; absolute reduction 3%). Clopidogrel pretreatment did not significantly reduce death, MI, or urgent target vessel revascularization at 28 days (reduction 18.5%; 95% CI -14.2% to 41.8%; P=.23).",
    "secondaryResults": "In a prespecified subgroup analysis, patients who received clopidogrel at least 6 hours before PCI had a relative risk reduction of 38.6% (95% CI -1.6% to 62.9%; P=.051) for the 28-day end point, with no reduction with treatment less than 6 hours before PCI.",
    "safety": "Major bleeding at 1 year increased, but not significantly (8.8% with clopidogrel versus 6.7% with placebo; P=.07).",
    "takeaway": "In 2116 patients undergoing or likely to undergo PCI, 12 months of clopidogrel added to aspirin reduced death, MI, or stroke at 1 year (26.9% relative reduction; absolute reduction 3%), while a clopidogrel loading dose before the procedure did not significantly reduce 28-day events.",
    "limitations": "The 28-day pretreatment analysis was in the per-protocol population, and the timing finding (at least 6 hours before PCI) comes from a prespecified subgroup analysis whose confidence interval crossed zero (P=.051). Major bleeding at 1 year rose, but not significantly (P=.07). The loading-dose and 12-month comparisons were combined in one design, so the 12-month result also reflects the loading dose group assignment.",
